@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Work Items, Epics, Features, and User Stories";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Azure Boards in Azure DevOps provides structured tools for managing work using **Work Items, Epics, Features, and User Stories**. These elements help teams track progress, prioritize tasks, and align development with business goals.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What are Work Items?</h2>
        <p>Work Items in Azure DevOps represent **tasks, bugs, or any unit of work** that needs to be tracked and managed within a project. Work Items form the backbone of Agile project management by allowing teams to maintain clarity and accountability.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Each work item contains a **title, description, priority, status, and assigned user**.</li>
            <li>Work items can be **linked to code commits, pull requests, builds, and test cases** for full traceability.</li>
            <li>They follow an **assigned workflow** (New → Active → Resolved → Closed).</li>
            <li>Work items allow **teams to track progress** efficiently across various sprints.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/work-items.png" alt="Work Items in Azure Boards" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Managing Work Items in Azure DevOps</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Types of Work Items</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Task:</strong> A specific work unit assigned to a team member.</li>
                <li><strong>Bug:</strong> Tracks defects found in the application.</li>
                <li><strong>User Story:</strong> Represents a customer requirement.</li>
                <li><strong>Feature:</strong> Groups multiple User Stories into a larger capability.</li>
                <li><strong>Epic:</strong> Represents large-scale business objectives containing multiple Features.</li>
                <li><strong>Test Case:</strong> Defines testing scenarios and expected results.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Understanding Epics, Features, and User Stories</h2>
        <p>Azure Boards follows a **hierarchical structure** to manage work in a structured manner:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Epics:</strong> High-level business initiatives that represent major strategic goals.</li>
            <li><strong>Features:</strong> Functional components that fulfill an Epic and are broken down into User Stories.</li>
            <li><strong>User Stories:</strong> Detailed requirements focused on end-user needs, written from a user's perspective.</li>
            <li><strong>Tasks:</strong> Work units required to complete a User Story, such as coding, testing, and documentation.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Example Work Item Hierarchy</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Epic:</strong> Improve User Authentication</li>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Feature:</strong> Implement Two-Factor Authentication</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>User Story:</strong> Allow users to enable two-factor authentication.</li>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Task:</strong> Create UI for authentication settings.</li>
                            <li><strong>Task:</strong> Implement SMS authentication method.</li>
                            <li><strong>Task:</strong> Integrate with authentication APIs.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/work-item-hierarchy.png" alt="Work Item Hierarchy" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Work Item hierarchy in Azure DevOps</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Managing Work Items in Azure Boards</h2>
        <p>Azure Boards provides various tools to **track, prioritize, and manage work** effectively:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Use **Backlogs** to prioritize Epics, Features, and User Stories effectively.</li>
            <li>Leverage **Sprint Planning** to assign tasks for the upcoming iteration.</li>
            <li>Track progress with **Kanban Boards**, **Burndown Charts**, and **Cumulative Flow Diagrams**.</li>
            <li>Integrate Work Items with **source code repositories** (Azure Repos, GitHub) for end-to-end traceability.</li>
            <li>Use **Queries and Dashboards** to generate reports and insights on project progress.</li>
            <li>Automate work item updates using **Azure DevOps rules and workflows**.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Best Practices for Managing Work Items</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Use **clear and concise titles** for Work Items to improve readability.</li>
            <li>Keep descriptions **detailed** and include **acceptance criteria**.</li>
            <li>Regularly update **status** to keep teams informed.</li>
            <li>Link Work Items to **code commits, builds, and test cases** for full traceability.</li>
            <li>Break down large Epics and Features into smaller, manageable User Stories.</li>
            <li>Use **queries and filters** to track high-priority Work Items.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Understanding Work Items, Epics, Features, and User Stories is crucial for organizing work efficiently. Azure Boards offers a structured framework to **prioritize, track, and deliver** value in Agile projects. By following best practices, teams can ensure better collaboration, increased transparency, and efficient development workflows.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to explore **Sprint Planning & Backlog Management** in Azure DevOps.
        </p>
    </div>

